44Z","last_update_date":"2024-11-24T20:18:38.395359Z","tool_type":["Desktop application"],"version":["1"],"collections":["BIG N2N","UGent","VIB"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:flowsom","credit":[{"email":"Yvan.Saeys@ugent.be","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"]},{"name":"ugent.be","typeEntity":"Institute","typeRole":["Provider"]}],"documentation":[{"type":["General"],"url":"https://github.com/SofieVG/FlowSOM"}],"edit_permission":{"authors":["bits@vib.be"],"type":"group"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Visualisation","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0337"}],"output":[{"data":{"term":"Pathway or network","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_2600"}}]}],"name":"FlowSOM","operating_system":["Linux","Mac","Windows"],"owner":"Katrijn.Vannerum@ugent.be","publication":[{"doi":"10.1002/cyto.a.22625","metadata":{"abstract":"The number of markers measured in both flow and mass cytometry keeps increasing steadily. Although this provides a wealth of information, it becomes infeasible to analyze these datasets manually. When using 2D scatter plots, the number of possible plots increases exponentially with the number of markers and therefore, relevant information that is present in the data might be missed. In this article, we introduce a new visualization technique, called FlowSOM, which analyzes Flow or mass cytometry data using a Self-Organizing Map. Using a two-level clustering and star charts, our algorithm helps to obtain a clear overview of how all markers are behaving on all cells, and to detect subsets that might be missed otherwise. R code is available at https://github.com/SofieVG/FlowSOM and will be made available at Bioconductor.","authors":[{"name":"Callebaut B."},{"name":"Demeester P."},{"name":"Dhaene T."},{"name":"Lambrecht B.N."},{"name":"Saeys Y."},{"name":"Van Gassen S."},{"name":"Van Helden M.J."}],"citationCount":1132,"date":"2015-07-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"Cytometry Part A","title":"FlowSOM: Using self-organizing maps for visualizati